150
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
and three types of leaders,

151
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
And then I've got an email question to answer

152
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
and that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

153
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
So let's get started, shall we

154
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Scoutmastership- in seven minutes or less,

155
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Now, any Scouter, anybody working with youth,

156
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
at one minute may be a coach

157
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
at next. they may be instructor.

158
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
a little later on they may be a counselor

159
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
or a manager, or a leader or a trainer.

160
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
The common thread woven to all these roles

161
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
is the spirit of a helpful guide.

162
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Let me contrast two different types of guides.

163
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Imagine a bus tour of some important city somewhere

164
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and we're seated in the air-conditioned comfort

165
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
of a motor coach and we roll along.

166
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
the guide explains each landmark in detail

167
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
so we don't miss anything.

168
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
In fact, there's so much explaining-

169
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
and you know how, if you've ever been

170
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
on one of those tours before.

171
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
the guide's on a script and there's a lot of explaining

172
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
and there's not a whole lot of time left for questions,

173
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,000
and we sit there and we listen attentively

174
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:19,000
and we dutifully turn our heads to the left

175
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
and to the right and then the tour is over.

176
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Now contrast that tour with a hike

177
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
guided by a knowledgeable outdoors guide,

178
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Our group takes the lead and finds the trail.

179
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
where the trail branches,

180
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
The guide will tell us which way to go

181
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
if we can't figure it out on our own.

182
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
He doesn't mind if we stop now and again

183
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
and we admire a flower or take in the view,

184
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
and he'll happily tell us what we're looking at

185
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
if we ask. but he's not going to push us along,

186
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
He's going to let us take our own pace

187
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
And though the guide may drop a hint here and there,

188
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
he's going to volunteer very little information.

189
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
We may miss some sites along the way

190
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
or we may pass by interesting things,

191
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
but we will probably get more out

192
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
of what we discovered on the hike

193
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
than the things that the tour guide

194
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
told us about on the bus.

195
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Do you see what I mean?

196
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Like the guide,

197
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
scouts are more responsive

198
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
than directive.

199
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
We inspire and encourage scouts

200
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
to explore and learn at their own speed.

201
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,000
Guiding scouts is more of a gentle push

202
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
in the right direction,

203
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
more of a suggestion rather than a command,

204
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
more of a question asked

205
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
rather than an answer given.

206
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
We can better understand

207
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
that special kind of leadership required

208
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
to be a good guide

209
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
in three different leadership styles,

210
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And to do that

211
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
I want to read you two quotes.

212
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
The first is from Baden Powell,

213
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
the founder of Scouting.

214
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
He said

215
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
leadership is the key note to success.

216
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
but leadership is difficult to define

217
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
And leaders are difficult to find.

218
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
I frequently stated that

219
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
any man can be a commander

220
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
and a trained man may often

221
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:03,000
make an instructor,

222
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
but a leader is more like a poet,

223
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
not manufactured.

224
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
The second quote I want to read you

225
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
is a very old quote from Laosu.

226
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
The best leader

227
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
is one that people are

228
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
barely aware of.

229
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
The next best is one who is

230
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
loved and praised by the people.

231
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
Next comes one

232
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
who is feared.

233
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Worst is one who is despised.

234
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
If the leader does not have

235
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
enough faith in his people,

236
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
they will have no faith in him.

237
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
He puts great value in words

238
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and says little,

239
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
so that when his work is finished

240
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
the people all say:

241
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
we did it ourselves.

242
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
Now, Baden Powell and Laosu

243
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
lived on opposite sides of the world

244
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
and wrote centuries apart.

245
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
but they both describe

246
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
three distinct leadership styles

247
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
And you know I'm going to define

248
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
some styles here. but it doesn't mean

249
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
that we're not going to define

250
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
some aspect of all three styles.

251
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
but the first style of leadership

252
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
is authoritarian leadership.

253
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
This is Laosu's feared leader

254
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and Baden Powell's commander

255
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
I can remember as a young director

256
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
of a program department

257
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
at our summer camp

258
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
I was kind of insufferable

259
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and authoritarian.

260
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
I was certainly demanding.

261
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
I wasn't particularly friendly

262
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
and I mistook my kind of abrasive personality

263
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
for clarity of mission.

264
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
I was brash

265
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
in the editorial

266
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and I really wonder that any of the counselors

267
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
that were working for me lasted through the summer.

268
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
I did get my comeuppance

269
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
at the end of the season

270
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
when one of them had the sand

271
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
to write exactly what he thought

272
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
of me and my job

273
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
in his evaluation at the end of the year,

274
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
And since then I've always considered

275
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
that I have owed him

276
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
a great debt of gratitude

277
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
because it gave me a chance

278
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
to look at myself and it changed me for the better.

279
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Authoritarian leaders

280
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
do get things done,

281
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
but our goal as

282
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
scouters is much broader

283
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
than just efficiently completing tasks.

284
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
Scouting has well defined

285
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
expectations, but these

286
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
are not enforced in an

287
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
obedience oriented,

288
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
authoritarian manner.

289
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
We want our scouts to be obedient,

290
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
but we don't want them to be frightened.

291
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
What we want to do is encourage them to ask questions

292
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
and find their own direction

293
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and then respond by helping them

294
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
find the answers to make things

295
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
happen. The second kind

296
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
of leader I want to talk about

297
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
is what I would call a permissive leader.

298
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
Lao Zhu called this

299
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
the despised leader.

300
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
Let's correspond this with

301
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
Baden Powell's trained man.

302
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And what I mean by a permissive leader

303
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
is when I was hired to coach

304
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
a middle school soccer team

305
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
decades ago, I was

306
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
young enough to remember authoritarian

307
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
coaches and teachers that I really

308
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
didn't like,

309
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
And I resolved I would be the kind

310
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
of coach everybody liked-

311
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
The cool coach, the cool teacher.

312
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
I traded the discipline

313
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
and authority a coach

314
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
needs to have

315
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
for currying favor with the team, and the result was

316
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
a real mess.

317
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
If you're a teacher or a coach out there

318
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
and you've had a similar experience, you're laughing right now

319
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
because you know exactly what I'm talking about.

320
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
What my players needed was a coach,

321
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and

322
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
a coach who would give them structure and discipline.

323
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
They didn't need another friend.

324
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
They already had plenty of friends.

325
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Permissive leaders: they're not

326
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
usually very sure of themselves

327
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
and they look for reassurance in the approval

328
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
and friendship of their followers.

329
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
For all their effort, permissive leaders

330
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
don't earn respect or friendship.

331
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
More often than not, they end up

332
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
with people laughing at them behind their backs

333
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
in the way that Lao Zhu describes.

334
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Now the third type of leader

335
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
is a responsive leader.

336
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
This is Lao Zhu's

337
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
leader that the people are

338
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
barely aware of,

339
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and what Baden Powell called

340
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
a poet.

341
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
Responsive leadership is really kind of this

342
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
barely perceptible influence

343
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
that empowers people to do things

344
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
for themselves, By listening more than

345
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
talking, because,

346
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
remember, Lao Zhu said that this type

347
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
of leader values words and

348
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
uses them infrequently.

349
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
By exercising goodwill

350
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
and having an unshakable faith

351
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
in the people that were leading,

352
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
responsive leaders can create

353
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
an atmosphere of shared accomplishment

354
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
And, rather than dictating

355
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
their own wishes, responsive leaders

356
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
inspire others

357
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
to adopt high expectations.

358
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
Their example of service

359
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
is a powerful motivational

360
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
force. In difficulty,

361
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
they're going to respond

362
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
not with authoritarianism,

363
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
but with empathy and compassion.

364
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
Now I'll tell you, after many

365
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
fits and starts and years of trying,

366
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
I do actually find myself

367
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
being a responsive leader

368
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
more often than not. I know there's

369
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
still plenty of room for improvement,

370
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
but if I trade the kind of self

371
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
aggrandizing authority

372
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
and my desire to be appreciated

373
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
for an honest aspiration

374
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
to serve other people, I am happier,

375
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
and so are my scouts.

376
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
My ideal scouter practices

377
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
this kind of responsive leadership,

378
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
enabling scouts to do things for themselves

379
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
while offering an appropriate

380
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
amount of direction to assure they remain

381
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
safe and they get the most out of scouting.

382
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
I knew I could do. I knew I could do.

383
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
I knew I could do. I knew I could do, I knew I could do.

384
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
I need a letter.

385
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
send it by name

386
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Email. that is, folks.

387
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and here's an answer to one of your

388
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
emails.

389
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:13,000
So I got this really interesting email this week

390
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
and it started out by saying

391
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
our troop is doing summer camp this year

392
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
with two other troops and I thought,

393
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
wow, that's going to be cool. A relatively

394
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
small summer camp experience,

395
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
kind of one that the troops are doing

396
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
on their own. I think that's a really cool idea.

397
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
And then the author said

398
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
I'm in charge of 10 or so scouts

399
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
who will be attending my classes.

400
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and I thought, uh oh,

401
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
classes. well, we need to

402
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
talk about that. I'm wondering

403
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
if you have or know

404
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
of a resource that outlines

405
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
a teaching curriculum for tender foot

406
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
through first class rank

407
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
in a week. and I said, uh oh,

408
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
we're going to have to do

409
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
some thinking here. So in my reply

410
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
this is what I wrote- I said:

411
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
now you're going to summer camp, right,

412
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
we're not going to school.

413
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
we have schedules and teaching and tests

414
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
and reading assignments and classes and

415
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
curriculums. We're at camp,

416
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
so let's keep it camping.

417
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
So what happens when we go camping?

418
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
if you look at the requirements up to first class

419
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
rank, that's what happens.

420
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
that's what scouts do. We don't

421
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
hold classes and work on requirements.

422
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
scouts go camping

423
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
and in the course of camping,

424
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
they do things that complete the

425
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
requirements. This is really kind of a

426
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
deceptively simple distinction,

427
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
but one that's so important in scouting

428
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
and we all too

429
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
frequently miss this. This isn't just

430
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
some semantic nicety

431
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
of actually having classes, but calling

432
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
them something different. This is for real.

433
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
what opportunities

434
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
ought to be able for scouts at summer camp?

435
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
well, take a look at the requirements.

436
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
that's what needs to be available.

437
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
you're going to pitch some tents and some

438
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
tarps. you're going to plan, prepare and clean

439
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
up after a meal. you're going to take a hike, play

440
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
a game, build a tower, go swimming.

441
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
that's what scouts do. are you following

442
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
me? do you get it? it's not a list

443
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
of requirements. it's what you do when

444
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
you go camping. so let's take our

445
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
10 scouts and meet with them

446
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
in the morning and say: today you're going to build a fire

447
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
and cook your lunch. now, what do you need to

448
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
build a fire? what safety concerns do you

449
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
have? what tools are you going to need? do you know how to use

450
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
the tools? what kind of wood is going

451
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
to work best. how much do you need?

452
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
do you know how to cook in a Dutch oven?

453
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
do you think there's anything in the scout

454
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
handbook that would help you figure things out?

455
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
I'll tell you what, guys. I'll be back

456
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
in an hour with the food. I want

457
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
to leave in about five or ten minutes, so give me

458
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
a list of what you need. while I'm gone,

459
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
you guys figure out how to get a fire

460
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
set up, get the wood, get everything

461
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
you need and have everything ready

462
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
to light when I get back and I'll bring

463
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
the matches and then we'll

464
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
have lunch and then you go away

465
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
and let them at it. they have a scout handbook.

466
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
they have a bunch of questions to ask.

467
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
they have a task to do. they're going

468
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
to figure it out. they might get it right,

469
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
they might get it wrong, it doesn't really

470
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
matter. they're camping and they're

471
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
having fun. they're hanging out with their

472
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
buddies and they're learning. so you're

473
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
going to get back and you're going to take a look

474
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
and maybe there's a couple of corrections to be made

475
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
or maybe there's a little bit of knowledge to fill in

476
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
a lot of times. that is going to be taken

477
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
care of by asking more questions.

478
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
so, guys, how did that go? did you figure

479
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
things out? who did what?

480
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
where did you learn how to do that?

481
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
are you having trouble? get it started?

482
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
what would you stop doing or start

483
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
doing to make things work better? do you see

484
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
what I mean? and then you can cook lunch

485
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
and so you make whatever corrections

486
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
there need to be made or modifications

487
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
to what they've done.

488
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
we haven't sat them down and taught them

489
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
anything for even 30 seconds,

490
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
much less sat them down and had a class

491
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
with a curriculum involved.

492
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
the next day, let's say

493
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
we're going to go swimming. what should you be

494
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
thinking of? is there anything in the scout

495
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
handbook that may that you may want to know

496
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
about going swimming? what kind of an area

497
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
do you need for swimming? should there be

498
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
a lifeguard? see what I'm saying.

499
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
the next day, you can say: today

500
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
we're going on a hike. what do you need to bring?

501
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
where are we going? how will we get there?

502
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
do you need a map and compass?

503
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
did you bring one with you? do you know how

504
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
to use a map and compass together? does the

505
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
scout handbook say anything about using a map

506
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
and compass? I'll tell you what

507
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
I have- some things to do, and in an hour

508
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
I'll meet you at this spot that I've

509
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
marked right here on the map. do you guys have

510
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
any questions? so, instead of sitting

511
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
down and having a class and a curriculum

512
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
and a plan, let's

513
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
go camping and do the things

514
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
that scouts do. and then you know what's

515
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
going to happen: is, at one

516
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
point, the lights going to go on and one of the

517
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
scouts is going to ask you: you know, we went on

518
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
that hike, is that a requirement for

519
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
tenderfoot? and you can say: well, I don't

520
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
know. did you check tenderfoot? let's take a

521
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
look here. which requirement? do you think

522
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
that it is? read the requirement to me?

523
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
do you think that you met that requirement?

524
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
and now they're getting self motivated

525
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
because they've discovered: oh, these

526
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
things that we're doing, they're in the

527
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
book and they actually meet requirements,

528
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
and all I have to do is ask about them

529
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
and if I've done them, I can get them

530
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
signed off. and if I haven't really done

531
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
them, well, now I know how to

532
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
work on that. so, no classes,

533
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
please, just go camping.

534
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
